{
  "abstract": "Can bounded-width digit programs support dense no-three-in-line constructions after arbitrary deletion? We prove that every synchronous width-$q$ deterministic program reading paired base-$b$ digits, with $q$ and $b$ fixed, has exponentially decaying relative no-three capacity on exponentially large accepted slices. The transition tables may depend on both the digit position and the total depth. Consequently, every such support of $O(n)$ points in a square of side $n=b^k$ has maximum no-three subset size $O(n^{1-\\varepsilon})$ for some $\\varepsilon>0$, uniformly over its transition schedule and with no restriction on the selection rule. The proof partitions words by block-boundary states and partitions each block alphabet into parallel lines; every product cell lies on one physical line. A logarithmic directional-loss inequality gives a uniform finite bound. It forces width $\\Omega(\\sqrt{k/\\log k})$ for linear-size supports admitting dense selections. For one fixed regular language, a simple-cycle residue decomposition also gives relative capacity $O(1/k)$ on polynomially growing slices and relative vanishing along every sequence of unbounded slice sizes. A parabola example shows the polynomial rate is sharp. Finally, a $q$-state stationary automaton whose accepted slice is itself no-three has at most $\\sum_{h<q}b^{2h}$ points whenever $k>4q^2+7q$, forcing asymptotic state count at least $\\tfrac12\\sqrt{k}$ for dense direct output. Exact arithmetic controls accompany the self-contained proofs. These results constrain digit-support constructions; the unrestricted density-$1.8$ problem remains open.",
